   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the lien law, in relation to disclosing if the property\nsubject to lien is real property improved or to be improved with a single\nfamily dwelling",
    "basePrintNo": "S5491",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5491-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §9, Lien L",
    "lawSection": "Lien Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5491\n\nSPONSOR: HARCKHAM\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the lien law, in relation to disclos-\ning if the property subject to lien is real property improved or to be\nimproved with a single family dwelling\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to make clear on the face of mechanics' lien\nwhether the lien being filed is on a single family dwelling or not, as\nthe renewal for liens differ depending on that fact.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends section 9 of the lien law by adding a new detail that\nmust be included on the mechanics' lien documents filed in New York\nState, namely whether to lien is on real property improved or to be\nimproved with a single family dwelling or not.\n\nSection 2 sets the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe Mechanics' Lien Law as is currently written allows a contractor or\nsomebody who supplied work, labor or materials to a projects, to file a\nlien against the property owner if their bill is unpaid.  After the lien\nis filed on a single family home, the lien can only be foreclosed within\none year of its filing date. For other property a lien lasts for a year,\nbut then can be extended once and therefore can be foreclosed on before\ntwo years have passed. Lien documents currently filed are not required\nto distinguish between a single family dwelling or other property. This\nhas opened the door for a lien to be filed on a single family home and\nthen the assertion to be made that the lien can be extended into a\nsecond year, Requiring that mechanics' liens specify whether they are on\na single family dwelling or not will ensure that liens filed in New York\nState are filed against the correct property classification and that New\nYork families are better protected.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the State.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
